In this lesson we'll look at the families of quadrilaterals and the special properties that set each one apart. A quadrilateral is just a flat shape with four straight sides, and its interior angles always add up to three hundred and sixty degrees, because you can split it into two triangles. Now we build up the family by stacking extra rules on top of that basic four sides idea. A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel, and from there its opposite sides and opposite angles are equal. A rectangle is a parallelogram that adds four right angles, so its diagonals are equal and they bisect each other. A rhombus is a parallelogram that adds four equal sides, which makes its diagonals cross at right angles and bisect each other. A square is both, so it gets all those lovely properties at once. A trapezium is different, with only one pair of parallel sides called the bases, and a kite has two pairs of adjacent sides equal, with one diagonal sitting on an axis of symmetry. These rules show up everywhere, from folding umbrellas to car jacks. Now let's see it in action.